E. What singles out 36
====================================================================================================
  j <= 5000 with m_j triangular (Ljunggren): [0, 1, 2];   with N_j 3-smooth: [1, 2]
  The 3-smooth statement needs no search: N = T_n is 3-smooth iff n and n+1 are both 3-smooth, and
  consecutive 3-smooth numbers are (1,2), (2,3), (3,4), (8,9) (Levi ben Gerson), so T_n in {1, 3, 6, 36};
  the squares among these are 1 and 36.  Status: proved, not 'computed j <= 3000'.
  3-smooth triangular numbers with index < 2*10^6: [1, 3, 6, 36]
  3 | m_j <=> 2 | j: True ;  v2(m_j) = v2(j): True

====================================================================================================
F. Pythagorean-lift discriminants 4^k + 9^s: perfect squares, and never 2*square
====================================================================================================
  (k, s) with 1 <= k < 300, 1 <= s < 200 and 4^k + 9^s a perfect square: [(2, 1)]
  proof: primitive triple (2^k, 3^s, y) => 2^k = 2ab, 3^s = a^2 - b^2 with b = 1, a = 2^(k-1);
         (2^(k-1) - 1)(2^(k-1) + 1) = 3^s forces 2^(k-1) - 1 = 1.  So (2, 1) is the only cell, for all k, s.
  4^8 + 9^5 = 124585 (= D of Set_13 in scripts/pythagorean_lifts/README.md)

